<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In statistical physics, the multivariate hard-core model describes a system of particles, each of which receives its own fugacity.
In graph-theoretic language, the partition function of the model translates to the <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate independence polynomial</em>, i.e., the multiaffine generalisation of the independence polynomial, defined by
<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\coloneqq\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\coloneqq\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all independent sets in a graph <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]\coloneqq\{1,2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]\coloneqq\{1,2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We prove that for every simple graph <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\geq\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1+(d_{i}+1)\lambda_{i})^{1/(d_{i}+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})\geq\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1+(d_{i}+1)\lambda_{i})^{1/(d_{i}+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\dots,d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\dots,d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree sequence of <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This generalises a result of Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao, who proved the univariate case <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\dots=\lambda_{n}=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\dots=\lambda_{n}=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">We further conjecture that our inequality should generalise to other antiferromagnetic models and give some evidence in support of it. In particular, for <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i},\mu_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i},\mu_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a stronger inequality</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}I,J\in\mathcal{I}(G)\\
I\cap J=\emptyset\end{subarray}}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v}\prod_{u\in J}\mu_{u}\geq\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left(1+(d_{i}+1)(\lambda_{i}+\mu_{i})+d_{i}(d_{i}+1)\lambda_{i}\mu_{i}\right)^{1/(d_{i}+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}I,J\in\mathcal{I}(G)\\
I\cap J=\emptyset\end{subarray}}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v}\prod_{u\in J}\mu_{u}\geq\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left(1+(d_{i}+1)(\lambda_{i}+\mu_{i})+d_{i}(d_{i}+1)\lambda_{i}\mu_{i}\right)^{1/(d_{i}+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.4" class="ltx_p">which proves our conjecture for a multiaffine generalisation of the <em id="abstract1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semiproper colouring</em> partition function with two proper colours. The proof is formalised in the Lean 4 proof assistant using Harmonic Aristotle.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.5" class="ltx_p">Our key technical steps for both theorems were obtained by using a custom mathematical research agent built on top of Gemini Deep Think, which can be seen as a benchmark demonstrating that the current state-of-the-art language models can, in part, assist with mathematical research.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independence polynomials are a central object in both combinatorics and statistical physics.
In particular, its multiaffine generalisation, the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate independence polynomial</em>, defined by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})=\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})=\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\prod_{v\in I}\lambda_{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of independent sets in <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, has played an important role in bridging combinatorics and statistical physics.
Indeed, it is the <em id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partition function</em> of the multivariate hard-core model and furthermore, the <em id="S1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cluster expansion</em>, which relies on the multivariate Taylor expansion of <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, has been an important tool in modern combinatorics; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> for an overview.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With slight abuse of notation, write <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq Z_{G}(\lambda,\dots,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq Z_{G}(\lambda,\dots,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, the usual single-variate independence polynomial or, equivalently, the partition function of the hard-core model with fugacity <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our first main result is to give a general lower bound for <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> that works for any finite simple graph <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and any nonnegative reals <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})\in(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})^{V(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛌</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})\in(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})^{V(G)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{K_{d_{v}+1}}(\lambda_{v})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}=\prod_{v\in V(G)}((d_{v}+1)\lambda_{v}+1)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{K_{d_{v}+1}}(\lambda_{v})^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}=\prod_{v\in V(G)}((d_{v}+1)\lambda_{v}+1)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equality holds if and only if on each connected component of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, either

<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1.</span></span> <span id="S1.I1.i1.10" class="ltx_text">all <math id="S1.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">2.</span></span> <math id="S1.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.10" class="ltx_text">is constant and each component of <math id="S1.I1.i2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a clique.
</span></span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This generalises a theorem of Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Theorem 1.7]</cite>, who proved (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) for single-variate independence polynomials, i.e., the case <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. We also remark that the simplest case, when <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}=d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, was first proved by Cutler and Radcliffe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Corollary 2.3]</cite>.
It is worth noting that, unlike previous results, the inequality (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) for <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not an equality but an AM–GM-type inequality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A quick corollary of the inequality (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is a lower bound for the generalised independence polynomial <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda;\alpha)=\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\lambda^{\lvert I\rvert}\alpha^{e(I,\overline{I})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda;\alpha)=\sum_{I\in\mathcal{I}(G)}\lambda^{\lvert I\rvert}\alpha^{e(I,\overline{I})}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(I,\overline{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(I,\overline{I})</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of crossing edges between <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\setminus I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\setminus I</annotation></semantics></math>, a partition function of a <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-spin <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">antiferromagnetic</em> model, which we shall define later.
Then setting <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=\lambda\alpha^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=\lambda\alpha^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be nonnegative reals. Write <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\coloneqq\deg_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda;\alpha)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{K_{d_{v}+1}}(\lambda;\alpha)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}=\prod_{v\in V(G)}\bigl((d_{v}+1)\lambda\alpha^{d_{v}}+1\bigr)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda;\alpha)\geq\prod_{v\in V(G)}Z_{K_{d_{v}+1}}(\lambda;\alpha)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}=\prod_{v\in V(G)}\bigl((d_{v}+1)\lambda\alpha^{d_{v}}+1\bigr)^{\frac{1}{d_{v}+1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We suspect that this result generalises even further. To elaborate on what this would mean, let <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge-weighted graph possibly with loops. Let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hom(G,H)\coloneqq\sum_{\phi:V(G)\rightarrow V(H)}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\phi(u),\phi(v))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)\coloneqq\sum_{\phi:V(G)\rightarrow V(H)}\prod_{uv\in E(G)}H(\phi(u),\phi(v))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">be the (weighted) homomorphism count for <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the corresponding edge weight of <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the independence polynomial <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for positive rational values <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=q/p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=q/p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H^{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H^{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a suitably ‘blown-up’ copy of the graph <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\!\hbox to18.66pt{\vbox to8.98pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 5.50648pt\lower-1.2pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG