A particular case of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Conjecture 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> when <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> represents an antiferromagnetic <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ising model</em>, i.e., the <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{11}=H_{22}=B\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{11}=H_{22}=B\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{12}=H_{21}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{12}=H_{21}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, was conjectured by Davies an Leblanc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Conjecture 5(ii)]</cite>.
It can also be seen as an antiferromagnetic counterpart to another theorem of Sah, Sawhney, Stoner, and Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Theorem 1.14]</cite>, stating that the reverse inequality to (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 1.2 ‣ Conjecture 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) holds whenever <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ferromagnetic</em>, i.e., all the eigenvalues are nonnegative.</p>
