Let <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q+1}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q+1}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained from <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by attaching a loop to one vertex, which is a <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-spin antiferromagnetic model.
This is one of the most important <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-spin antiferromagnetic models, as the support of an antiferromagnetic graph is always a blow-up of <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q+1}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q+1}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> after removing isolated vertices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Theorem 3.2]</cite>.
Our goal is to obtain a slightly weaker bound than (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ 4 More towards ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) for these graphs.</p>
