<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Concluding remarks</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Conjecture 4.2. ‣ 4 More towards ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generalise <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> to prove <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Conjecture 4.2. ‣ 4 More towards ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, one should replace <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> by a <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-variate function <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{q})\coloneqq\sum_{k=0}^{q}(d)_{k}e_{k}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{q})\coloneqq\sum_{k=0}^{q}(d)_{k}e_{k}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d)_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d)_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the falling factorial <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d(d-1)\cdots(d-k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(d-1)\cdots(d-k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{k}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the elementary symmetric function of degree <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the inductive approach, it is natural to replace <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by the first-order derivatives of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{d}^{1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{d}^{1/d}</annotation></semantics></math> is again concave, and the dual set <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and its log-convexity generalises with no problems. However, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1 The dual set 𝒮_𝑑 and log-convexity ‣ 3 Semiproper colourings ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, which heavily relies on technical advantages in low dimensions including <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Proof of ‣ 3 Semiproper colourings ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, seems to be the bottleneck. More precisely, as <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> grows, it gets harder to compute <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{0},a_{1},\dots,a_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{0},a_{1},\dots,a_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> is in the boundary of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We believe that <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=4</annotation></semantics></math> would already require substantially new ideas, whereas it might be possible to handle the case <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math> with analogous techniques to ours.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extensions to negative fugacity</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a (hyper)graph of maximum degree <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, is a complex number with modulus at most <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\Delta^{\Delta}}{(\Delta+1)^{\Delta+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\Delta^{\Delta}}{(\Delta+1)^{\Delta+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Theorem 10]</cite> obtains a lower bound of the form</p>
<table id="S5.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})\geq\left(1-\frac{1}{\Delta+1}\right)^{\lvert V(G)\rvert}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})\geq\left(1-\frac{1}{\Delta+1}\right)^{\lvert V(G)\rvert}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As the right-hand side is smaller than <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, this is weaker than <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> for nonnegative values of the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Having seen this comparison, one might wonder whether the range of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>’s in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> extends further to negative, or even complex, values. Unfortunately, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> does not extend to such a range, which was also observed by Gemini 3.0 Deep Think.
To elaborate, it is worth noting that substituting the value of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that makes the partial derivative of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A+\lambda_{0})(1+(\Delta+1)\lambda_{0})^{-\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A+\lambda_{0})(1+(\Delta+1)\lambda_{0})^{-\frac{1}{\Delta+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish also gives the inequality (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 2.4 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2 Multivariate independence polynomials ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>). Thus, (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 2.3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2 Multivariate independence polynomials ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) holds if and only if (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 2.4 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2 Multivariate independence polynomials ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) holds. But then, analysing the Taylor expansion of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta+1)A-\Delta B^{\frac{\Delta+1}{\Delta}}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta+1)A-\Delta B^{\frac{\Delta+1}{\Delta}}-1</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=0</annotation></semantics></math> proves that (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 2.4 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2 Multivariate independence polynomials ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) does not hold locally for some small negative values of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Occupancy fractions</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When proving inequalities on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, or more generally on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hom(G,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hom(G,H)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">occupancy fraction method</em> is to compare <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log L(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log L(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is a candidate for the upper/lower bound for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq\frac{\lambda}{\lvert V(G)\rvert}\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq\frac{\lambda}{\lvert V(G)\rvert}\frac{d}{d\lambda}\log Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, the so-called <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">occupancy fraction</em>, has a clear combinatorial interpretation; it corresponds to the expected size of a randomly sampled independent set according to the hard-core model. The occupancy fraction method has seen a lot of success in proving combinatorial inequalities, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
One of the interesting conjectures in the area is due to Davies and Kang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, stating that the inequality</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{G}(\lambda)\geq\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{K_{d_{i}+1}}(\lambda)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\lambda}{1+(d_{i}+1)\lambda}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{G}(\lambda)\geq\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{K_{d_{i}+1}}(\lambda)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\lambda}{1+(d_{i}+1)\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">holds for all nonnegative <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and any simple graph <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not hard to see that this conjecture implies the Sah–Sawhney–Stoner–Zhao inequality, i.e., <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> with all <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although the regular case <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}=d</annotation></semantics></math> was settled by Davies, Jenssen, Perkins, and Roberts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Davies, Sandhu, and Tan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite> proved the irregular case provided <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\leq\frac{3}{(\Delta(G)+1)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\leq\frac{3}{(\Delta(G)+1)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the conjecture remains open.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Having seen the Davies–Kang conjecture, it seems plausible to look for a strengthening of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Lower bounds for multivariate independence polynomials and their generalisations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in terms of occupancy fractions.
To elaborate, for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(\bm{\lambda})\coloneqq\lambda_{v}\frac{\partial}{\partial{\lambda_{v}}}\log Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(\bm{\lambda})\coloneqq\lambda_{v}\frac{\partial}{\partial{\lambda_{v}}}\log Z_{G}(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> be the <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertex occupancy</em> or <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertex marginal</em> at <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the probability that <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is occupied by an independent set randomly sampled according to the multivariate hard-core model.
For <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
